WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.275
Take two lines through the

2
00:00:05.366 --> 00:00:07.192
origin of the plane.

3
00:00:07.192 --> 00:00:09.932
Line V points along two, one.

4
00:00:09.932 --> 00:00:13.220
Line W points along minus one, one.

5
00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:17.575
Add any vector of V to any vector of W.

6
00:00:17.575 --> 00:00:21.784
Which points of the plane can you reach?

7
00:00:21.784 --> 00:00:23.530
Pause and guess.

8
00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:29.621
The sum V plus W is the

9
00:00:29.731 --> 00:00:32.024
set of all such sums.

10
00:00:32.024 --> 00:00:36.173
For example, two, one plus minus one

11
00:00:36.173 --> 00:00:39.121
one is the point one, two.

12
00:00:39.121 --> 00:00:43.270
The parallelogram shows the addition.

13
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:47.338
Every point of the plane splits this way.

14
00:00:47.338 --> 00:00:51.147
Minus three, zero is minus one times two

15
00:00:51.147 --> 00:00:54.412
one, plus one times minus one, one.

16
00:00:54.412 --> 00:00:56.317
The split is unique

17
00:00:56.317 --> 00:01:00.035
because the two lines meet only at zero.

18
00:01:00.035 --> 00:01:02.030
That is a direct sum

19
00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:04.660
written with a circled plus.

20
00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.629
Lecture ten gave the main examples.

21
00:01:07.629 --> 00:01:09.500
For a one by two matrix

22
00:01:09.575 --> 00:01:11.895
the row space and the nullspace

23
00:01:11.969 --> 00:01:13.017
are two lines

24
00:01:13.017 --> 00:01:15.861
they split the plane as a direct sum

25
00:01:15.861 --> 00:01:18.330
and they are even perpendicular.

26
00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:22.139
Dimensions follow a counting rule.

27
00:01:22.234 --> 00:01:26.223
The dimension of V plus W is the dimension

28
00:01:26.318 --> 00:01:29.072
of V plus the dimension of W

29
00:01:29.072 --> 00:01:32.492
minus the dimension of the overlap.

30
00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:36.575
Here that is one plus one minus zero: two

31
00:01:36.575 --> 00:01:38.190
the whole plane.

32
00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:41.555
Now let W be the same line as V.

33
00:01:41.555 --> 00:01:44.382
Every sum stays on that line.

34
00:01:44.382 --> 00:01:47.303
One plus one minus one is one

35
00:01:47.303 --> 00:01:50.224
the overlap was counted twice

36
00:01:50.224 --> 00:01:52.580
so we take it away once.

37
00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.648
A tempting shortcut

38
00:01:54.722 --> 00:01:56.954
the dimension of a sum is just

39
00:01:57.029 --> 00:01:58.889
the two dimensions added.

40
00:01:58.889 --> 00:02:00.600
Does that always work?

41
00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:05.645
Take two planes in three-dimensional space.

42
00:02:05.645 --> 00:02:09.902
V is spanned by one, zero, one and zero

43
00:02:09.902 --> 00:02:10.941
one, one.

44
00:02:10.941 --> 00:02:15.198
W is spanned by one, one, zero and zero

45
00:02:15.198 --> 00:02:16.340
zero, one.

46
00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:19.101
Put all four vectors as the

47
00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:21.155
columns of one matrix.

48
00:02:21.155 --> 00:02:23.745
Its rank is three, not four

49
00:02:23.834 --> 00:02:27.852
so the sum is all of three-dimensional space.

50
00:02:27.852 --> 00:02:30.888
The two planes must share a line

51
00:02:30.888 --> 00:02:33.746
the line through one, one, two.

52
00:02:33.746 --> 00:02:37.050
Three equals two plus two minus one.

53
00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:41.620
Now a different way to build bigger objects

54
00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:44.068
arrays with three indices.

55
00:02:44.068 --> 00:02:46.608
Two users rate three films

56
00:02:46.608 --> 00:02:48.784
in each of two seasons.

57
00:02:48.784 --> 00:02:50.960
That is twelve numbers

58
00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:53.590
a two by three by two array.

59
00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.399
Fix the season

60
00:02:55.399 --> 00:02:58.512
and you get an ordinary matrix

61
00:02:58.512 --> 00:03:00.069
called a slice.

62
00:03:00.069 --> 00:03:04.057
Season one is four, zero, two, over two

63
00:03:04.057 --> 00:03:05.127
zero, one.

64
00:03:05.127 --> 00:03:09.407
Season two is five, one, three, over three

65
00:03:09.407 --> 00:03:10.380
one, two.

66
00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.442
The simplest such array is called rank one.

67
00:03:14.442 --> 00:03:17.844
Each entry is a product of three numbers

68
00:03:17.844 --> 00:03:19.787
one from a user vector

69
00:03:19.787 --> 00:03:21.731
one from a film vector

70
00:03:21.731 --> 00:03:24.160
and one from a season vector.

71
00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:26.693
Our ratings are the sum of

72
00:03:26.777 --> 00:03:28.456
two of these pieces.

73
00:03:28.456 --> 00:03:31.562
In the first, user weights two, one

74
00:03:31.562 --> 00:03:34.501
times film weights two, zero, one

75
00:03:34.501 --> 00:03:36.600
give these six products

76
00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.782
the same in both seasons.

77
00:03:38.782 --> 00:03:42.392
The second piece adds one to every rating

78
00:03:42.392 --> 00:03:44.407
but only in season two.

79
00:03:44.407 --> 00:03:46.590
So four plus one is five.

80
00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:50.594
To use matrix tools, unfold the array.

81
00:03:50.685 --> 00:03:53.028
Lay the columns of the two

82
00:03:53.118 --> 00:03:56.812
slices side by side: film one season one

83
00:03:56.812 --> 00:03:59.696
film one season two, and so on.

84
00:03:59.696 --> 00:04:02.850
One row per user, and six columns.

85
00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:05.559
This unfolding has rank two

86
00:04:05.643 --> 00:04:08.592
because each rank-one piece unfolds

87
00:04:08.676 --> 00:04:10.698
to a matrix of rank one.

88
00:04:10.698 --> 00:04:13.984
Tensor rank in general is much harder

89
00:04:13.984 --> 00:04:16.680
there is no elimination for it.

90
00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:20.761
Contraction sums over a shared index.

91
00:04:20.856 --> 00:04:24.262
Give the first season weight one and

92
00:04:24.356 --> 00:04:27.952
the second season weight two, and add

93
00:04:27.952 --> 00:04:31.642
the first slice plus twice the second.

94
00:04:31.642 --> 00:04:35.899
That gives fourteen, two, eight, over eight

95
00:04:35.899 --> 00:04:36.940
two, five.

96
00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.940
Averaging over the two users as

97
00:04:40.025 --> 00:04:43.701
well gives one score per film: eleven, two

98
00:04:43.701 --> 00:04:45.410
and six and a half.

99
00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:47.350
In index notation

100
00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:50.265
the inputs are labelled by user

101
00:04:50.265 --> 00:04:53.622
film and season, and by season alone

102
00:04:53.622 --> 00:04:56.448
the output keeps user and film.

103
00:04:56.537 --> 00:04:59.187
Any label that is missing from

104
00:04:59.275 --> 00:05:01.483
the output gets added up.

105
00:05:01.572 --> 00:05:04.487
Attention scores in a transformer

106
00:05:04.575 --> 00:05:06.783
are the same kind of sum

107
00:05:06.872 --> 00:05:09.257
one matrix product for each

108
00:05:09.345 --> 00:05:11.200
example in the batch.

109
00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:14.696
Sums of subspaces add dimensions

110
00:05:14.696 --> 00:05:16.375
minus the overlap.

111
00:05:16.375 --> 00:05:18.672
When the overlap is zero

112
00:05:18.761 --> 00:05:21.146
the sum is direct and every

113
00:05:21.235 --> 00:05:23.267
vector splits uniquely.

114
00:05:23.267 --> 00:05:26.625
Arrays with more indices have slices

115
00:05:26.625 --> 00:05:29.452
rank-one pieces and unfoldings

116
00:05:29.452 --> 00:05:33.340
and a contraction sums over a shared index.

117
00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:34.773
Next elective

118
00:05:34.773 --> 00:05:37.711
Gaussians and weighted least squares

119
00:05:37.711 --> 00:05:40.650
where a matrix describes uncertainty.
